    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is the best time to take the uric acid test?",
        "answer": "The test can be done at any time during the day. However, it's often performed in the morning when included with other tests that may require fasting."
      },
      {
        "question": "Is overnight fasting mandatory for the uric acid test, and for how long?",
        "answer": "Fasting is generally not required for a standalone uric acid test. However, if it's part of a larger panel, you may need to fast for 8 to 12 hours."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can dehydration affect my uric acid test results?",
        "answer": "Yes, dehydration can make your blood more concentrated, potentially leading to falsely elevated uric acid levels. Stay well-hydrated before your test unless instructed otherwise by your doctor."
      },
      {
        "question": "How should I prepare my diet and medications before the uric acid test?",
        "answer": "Avoid alcohol and high-purine foods like red meat and organ meats for 24 hours before testing. Inform your doctor about all medications, as some may affect results."
      },
      {
        "question": "How is the blood draw procedure performed for the uric acid test?",
        "answer": "A healthcare professional cleans your arm, applies a tourniquet, inserts a sterile needle into your vein, and collects 2 ml of blood. The entire process takes just minutes."
